language variant
language en-us
name kitt
gender male 35

// CLASSIC, PRESSED TWICE — the car from Knight Rider.
//
// The counterpart to hal, and deliberately its opposite in every dimension that
// hal commits to. Both are machines that speak like people; they are not the
// same KIND of person. hal is warm, slow and never emphasises anything. This one
// is bright, quick, precise and faintly superior — a very capable assistant who
// is being patient with you, and would like that noticed.
//
// Same base (en-us) so the two are heard as one character's two moods rather
// than as two accents.

// HIGHER AND BRIGHTER, WITH ROOM TO BE WRY.
// hal is 108/132 — the narrowest band in the cast. This is 132/186: up a fourth
// and nearly twice the range, because this voice INFLECTS. The dryness is in the
// timing and the consonants, not in a flat line; a wry remark needs somewhere to
// go, and hal deliberately has nowhere.
pitch 132 186

// FORWARD, NOT WARM. The peak moves up to 1.5 kHz — out of the chest and into
// the mouth — and the top stays open where hal's is rolled off hard. That
// forwardness is the whole difference in colour: hal sounds like he is in the
// room with you, this sounds like it is coming out of a dashboard, and it is.
// The slight lift at 3 kHz is what reads as electronic without being a robot.
tone 320 165 1500 245 2400 200 3000 170 4200 110

// A shorter, brighter tract: F2 and F3 up, which is what makes a voice sound
// like it is smiling slightly even when it is not.
formant 0  98 116 116
formant 1 102 118 122
formant 2 110 124 126
formant 3 112 118 118
formant 4 108 110  98
formant 5 100  98  78
formant 6  90  88  52
formant 7  84  82  36
formant 8  80  78  26

// NO BREATH, AND A FAINT EDGE.
// hal's generosity of air is what makes him sound at ease. This voice has almost
// none: it is not relaxed, it is EFFICIENT. roughness 1 puts the faintest grain
// on it — the trace of a circuit rather than a throat — and flutter stays 0,
// because it is no more capable of wavering than hal is.
breath 0 1 1 1 1 1 1 0
breathw 60 75 85 85 85 85 75 60
voicing 88
roughness 1
flutter 0

// CRISP. Every consonant struck cleanly and released. hal articulates like
// someone with all the time in the world; this articulates like someone who has
// already finished the sentence and is waiting for you to catch up.
consonants 92 88

// Almost no room at all — a small hard cabin, close-miked. 26 ms is below the
// threshold the ear reads as an echo, so it registers as a surface rather than
// a space.
echo 26 10

// EMPHASIS, AND PLENTY OF IT.
// hal leans on nothing, which is his whole character. This leans on quite a lot:
// stressAdd climbs steeply and stressAmp with it, so the voice points at the
// important word and expects you to have noticed. That is the difference between
// something unnerving and something helpful.
intonation 2
stressAmp   15 16 17 19 21 24 27 30
stressAdd    0  8 18 30 44 60  88 120
